  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R2 already combines nanoindentation with temperature-dependent tribological testing of an HEA film, while R3 supplies elevated-temperature HEA friction and wear testing. R4 and R13 establish temperature-controlled atomistic mechanical simulations in FCC HEAs and connect high temperature to reduced strength, structural transformation, HCP evolution, and instability. R16 directly links temperature to stacking-fault energetics and FCC/HCP competition in a closely related Cantor-type alloy, and R1, R7, R8, and R15 provide complementary temperature-dependent dislocation and softening behavior. These teachings could reasonably motivate temperature-resolved MD nanoindentation/friction of an FCC HEA coating and analysis through disorder, dislocations, stacking faults, and HCP atoms. The precise FeNiCrCoCu/Cu geometry, sharp 900 K threshold, dislocation-density reduction, and increasing friction are not individually disclosed—and R3 reports the opposite friction trend due to oxide formation—but they represent system-specific outcomes rather than a wholly new experimental or mechanistic concept."
